---
components: ["Heatmap"]
description: Heatmap colors the cells of a table by value, to show where two dimensions run high together.
---

<script>
  import { Heatmap } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const DAYS = ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"];
  const BLOCKS = ["00–04", "04–08", "08–12", "12–16", "16–20", "20–24"];
  const SHAPE = [4, 9, 58, 66, 41, 17];
  const requests = DAYS.flatMap((day, d) =>
    BLOCKS.map((block, b) => ({
      day,
      block,
      requests: Math.round(SHAPE[b] * (d > 4 ? 0.35 : 1) * (1 + ((d * 7 + b * 3) % 5) / 10) * 100),
    })),
  ).filter((row) => !(row.day === "Sun" && row.block === "00–04"));

  const METRICS = ["Latency", "Errors", "Traffic", "Saturation"];
  const R = [
    [1, 0.72, 0.31, 0.64],
    [0.72, 1, -0.12, 0.48],
    [0.31, -0.12, 1, -0.55],
    [0.64, 0.48, -0.55, 1],
  ];
  const correlation = METRICS.flatMap((a, i) => METRICS.map((b, j) => ({ a, b, r: R[i][j] })));

  let selected = null;
</script>

`Heatmap` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

For a single row of cells, use [HeatStrip](/components/HeatStrip).

## Basic

Pass long `data` with `x`, `y`, and `value` accessors. Rows and columns keep first-seen order, rows that land on the same cell are summed, and a cell with no data is hatched so it cannot be read as a low value.

<Heatmap data={requests} x="day" y="block" value="requests" title="Requests by day and time" rowHeader="Time" />

## Cell labels

Set `cellLabels` to write each value in its cell. The label color follows the fill, so it stays readable across the ramp. `format` takes `Intl.NumberFormat` options or a function.

<Heatmap data={requests} x="day" y="block" value="requests" title="Requests by day and time" cellLabels />

## Order

Set `xOrder` and `yOrder` to fix the order. Keys outside the list are dropped, and keys with no data still get their row or column.

<Heatmap
  data={requests}
  x="day"
  y="block"
  value="requests"
  xOrder={["Sat", "Sun"]}
  yOrder={["08–12", "12–16", "16–20"]}
  title="Weekend daytime"
  cellLabels
/>

## Palette and domain

`palette` takes a sequential hue (`"blue"`, `"purple"`, `"cyan"`, `"teal"`) or a diverging palette (`"red-cyan"`, `"purple-teal"`). A diverging palette is centered on the middle of the `domain`, so set the domain when the midpoint matters, as it does for a correlation matrix.

<Heatmap
  data={correlation}
  x="a"
  y="b"
  value="r"
  palette="red-cyan"
  domain={[-1, 1]}
  format={{ maximumFractionDigits: 2 }}
  cellLabels
  size="lg"
  title="Correlation between signals"
/>

## Sizes

`size` sets the cell height: 24, 32, or 48 pixels. Columns share the width equally.

<Heatmap data={requests} x="day" y="block" value="requests" palette="teal" size="sm" title="Small cells" />

## Selectable

Set `selectable` to turn cells into toggle buttons. Bind `selected` or listen for `select`, whose detail holds the cell and the `data` rows summed into it.

<Heatmap
  data={requests}
  x="day"
  y="block"
  value="requests"
  title="Pick a time slot"
  selectable
  bind:selected
/>

## Accessibility

The heatmap is a table: the `title` is its caption, and every cell has a row header and a column header. Set `rowHeader` to name the header column. Each cell holds its value as text even when `cellLabels` is off, so nothing rests on color alone. When `selectable`, the grid is one tab stop and the arrow keys move between cells, skipping empty ones. The legend is hidden from assistive technology because the cells already carry their values.
